##
    Where a name lives, and how long it lives there.

    Three things that shape a program without appearing in its output: a block that exists only
    to bound a name, a function declared inside another and able to see what surrounds it, and
    the word that says how far a type can be seen.

    Nothing here is exotic. It is the machinery every other sample sits on, gathered in one
    place so it can be watched.
##

##
    'internal' is what a type says when it should be reachable anywhere in its project and
    nowhere beyond it. A type that says nothing is internal already — writing it is for when a
    reader should not have to know the default.
##
internal model Ledger

    integer[] amounts;

    public function Ledger()
        this.amounts = {};
    end function

    public function Add(integer amount)
        this.amounts.Insert(amount);
    end function

    public integer function Total()
        integer running = 0;

        for each amount in this.amounts
            running = running + amount;
        end for

        yield running;
    end function
end model

global model Program

    function Main()
        Program.Blocks();
        Program.NestedFunctions();
        Program.Capturing();

        Ledger ledger = new Ledger();

        ledger.Add(40);
        ledger.Add(2);

        Console.WriteLine("== a type only this project can see ==");
        Console.WriteLine("  total: " + ledger.Total());
    end function

    ##
        'begin' opens a scope and nothing else. It runs no loop and asks no question — it draws
        a line around a name so the name ends at the line.

        Useful when two stretches of a function each want a working value and neither should
        reach the other's. Outside the block that name is free again.
    ##
    function Blocks()
        Console.WriteLine("== a block that only bounds a name ==");

        begin
            integer scratch = 6 * 7;
            Console.WriteLine("  inside:  " + scratch);
        end

        begin
            string scratch = "a different one entirely";
            Console.WriteLine("  inside:  " + scratch);
        end

        # Neither 'scratch' reaches here, and the two never met each other.
        Console.WriteLine("  outside: neither name survives the block");
    end function

    ##
        A function may be declared inside another. Its name is reachable only within the
        function that holds it, which is what to reach for when a helper is meaningful to one
        caller and to nothing else.

        Both forms are here: one that yields a result, and one that yields nothing.
    ##
    function NestedFunctions()
        Console.WriteLine("== a function inside a function ==");

        integer function Doubled(integer value)
            yield value * 2;
        end function

        function Announce(string label, integer value)
            Console.WriteLine("  " + label + ": " + value);
        end function

        Announce("doubled 4", Doubled(4));
        Announce("doubled 21", Doubled(21));
    end function

    ##
        A nested function sees the locals around it, and sees them as they are rather than as
        they were when it was written. The counter below is read at each call, not copied at
        the declaration.
    ##
    function Capturing()
        Console.WriteLine("== what a nested function can see ==");

        integer counted = 0;

        function Count()
            counted = counted + 1;
        end function

        Count();
        Count();
        Count();

        Console.WriteLine("  counted: " + counted);
    end function

end model
